var e=JSON.parse(`"---\\ntitle: \\"Instagram Post Size Guide: Dimensions and Aspect Ratios\\"\\ndescription: \\"The complete guide to Instagram post sizes. Learn the exact dimensions, pixels, and aspect ratios for square, portrait, landscape posts, and carousels.\\"\\nquickAnswerTitle: \\"Quick Answer\\"\\nquickAnswer:\\n  - \\"The optimal size for an Instagram carousel is Portrait: 1080x1350 pixels (4:5 ratio).\\"\\n  - \\"Portrait posts take up maximum screen real estate, increasing read time and engagement.\\"\\n  - \\"The classic Square size is 1080x1080 pixels (1:1 ratio), which matches the profile grid display.\\"\\n  - \\"The first image in a multi-image carousel sets the forced aspect ratio for all subsequent slides.\\"\\n  - \\"GoToFlow automatically generates visual carousels in the perfect 4:5 aspect ratio without manual resizing.\\"\\nslug: \\"instagram-post-size-guide\\"\\nprimaryKeyword: \\"instagram post size guide\\"\\ncanonical: \\"https://gotoflow.io/blog/instagram-post-size-guide\\"\\nlanguage: \\"en\\"\\nclusterId: \\"en:instagram-carousel\\"\\nrelatedProductRoute: \\"/instagram-carousel-maker\\"\\npublished: true\\nnoindex: false\\narticleType: \\"workflow_article\\"\\nproductFit: \\"YES\\"\\nproductFitExplanation: \\"This guide covers Instagram formatting. GoToFlow handles carousel formatting automatically.\\"\\nrequiredVisualBlock: \\"tutorial\\"\\nfaqFormat: \\"structured\\"\\nqualityGateStatus: \\"passed\\"\\nlastReviewed: \\"2026-06-21\\"\\ncreatedAt: \\"2026-06-21\\"\\nupdatedAt: \\"2026-10-07\\"\\nexplore:\\n  tools:\\n    - title: \\"AI Instagram Post Generator\\"\\n      href: \\"/instagram-carousel-maker\\"\\n      description: \\"Create correctly sized Instagram visual content with AI.\\"\\n    - title: \\"AI Carousel Generator\\"\\n      href: \\"/instagram-carousel-maker\\"\\n      description: \\"Turn an idea or source into a complete carousel workflow.\\"\\nfaq:\\n  - question: \\"What is the best Instagram post size?\\"\\n    answer: \\"The portrait size (1080 x 1350 pixels, 4:5 aspect ratio) is generally the best. It takes up the maximum vertical space on a smartphone screen, keeping users focused on your content longer.\\"\\n  - question: \\"What is the Instagram Carousel size?\\"\\n    answer: \\"An Instagram carousel can use any standard post size (square, portrait, or landscape). However, the portrait size (1080 x 1350 pixels) is highly recommended for carousels to maximize screen real estate and readability.\\"\\n  - question: \\"Why does Instagram crop my photos?\\"\\n    answer: \\"Instagram automatically crops photos that don't fit within its supported aspect ratios (between 1.91:1 and 4:5). To avoid awkward cropping, resize your images to one of the standard dimensions before uploading.\\"\\n  - question: \\"What size should Instagram profile pictures be?\\"\\n    answer: \\"Your Instagram profile picture is displayed at 110 x 110 pixels on mobile, but it's best to upload a 320 x 320 pixel image to ensure it looks sharp across all devices.\\"\\n  - question: \\"Can a carousel have mixed sizes?\\"\\n    answer: \\"No. The size and aspect ratio of the very first slide in your carousel will dictate the crop for all subsequent slides in that post. Always ensure your slides have uniform dimensions.\\"\\nfinalCta:\\n  title: \\"Stop struggling with Instagram dimensions\\"\\n  text: \\"Use GoToFlow to generate correctly sized, ready-to-publish Instagram carousels in minutes.\\"\\n  buttonText: \\"Create Instagram Carousel\\"\\n  primaryHref: \\"/instagram-carousel-maker\\"\\n  microcopy: \\"Free — No credit card required\\"\\n  secondaryText: \\"Create an Instagram post with AI →\\"\\n  secondaryHref: \\"/instagram-carousel-maker\\"\\n---\\n\\nUsing the exact Instagram post size is critical for both visual quality and algorithm engagement. If your image is too small, it will look pixelated when compressed. If the aspect ratio is wrong, Instagram will aggressively crop out crucial parts of your photo or text, ruining your design. \\n\\nFor creators and brands publishing multi-slide carousels or text-heavy graphics, understanding exact dimensions is what separates a professional feed from an amateur one. This guide covers the exact pixel requirements for every feed format.\\n\\n## What you need to know\\n\\n- **Best for engagement:** Portrait (1080 x 1350 px, 4:5 aspect ratio). This dominates the mobile screen.\\n- **Classic format:** Square (1080 x 1080 px, 1:1 aspect ratio). Reliable and predictable on the profile grid.\\n- **Maximum width:** Landscape (1080 x 566 px, 1.91:1 aspect ratio). Hardest to succeed with in a fast-scrolling feed.\\n- **Carousel Rule:** The first image in a carousel strictly sets the aspect ratio for the rest of the slides.\\n- **Resolution matters:** Always aim for a width of exactly 1080 pixels to avoid compression artifacts.\\n\\n> **Don't waste time formatting slides manually**\\n> GoToFlow automatically generates educational carousels in the perfect 4:5 aspect ratio for Instagram, so you never have to worry about pixels, safe zones, or awkward cropping.\\n> [Try Instagram Carousel Maker](/instagram-carousel-maker)\\n\\n## The 3 Main Instagram Post Sizes\\n\\nInstagram supports three primary image orientations for the feed. Here is the exact breakdown of dimensions and when to use them.\\n\\n### 1. Portrait (Vertical) Size\\n- **Dimensions:** 1080 x 1350 pixels\\n- **Aspect Ratio:** 4:5\\n\\nPortrait is the undisputed king of Instagram feed posts. Because most users browse the app on vertical smartphone screens, a 4:5 image takes up nearly the entire screen. This forces the user to pause their scroll, blocking out distractions from the post above or below yours. It is highly recommended for infographics, text-heavy posts, and multi-slide carousels.\\n\\n:::mockup{slot=\\"format-settings\\"}\\n\\n### 2. Square Size\\n- **Dimensions:** 1080 x 1080 pixels\\n- **Aspect Ratio:** 1:1\\n\\nThe square format is Instagram’s original layout. While it doesn't take up as much vertical space as a portrait post, it remains a reliable and clean format. Square posts are particularly easy to manage because they look identical in the feed and on your profile grid (which displays all posts as 1:1 squares).\\n\\n### 3. Landscape (Horizontal) Size\\n- **Dimensions:** 1080 x 566 pixels\\n- **Aspect Ratio:** 1.91:1\\n\\nLandscape images are the most difficult to execute well on Instagram. They take up very little vertical space, meaning users can easily scroll past them in a fraction of a second. This format is typically reserved for uncropped photography or wide panoramic shots where the full width is absolutely necessary to tell the visual story.\\n\\n> [!related]\\n> Looking for content inspiration for these formats? Check out our [Social Media Post Ideas](/blog/social-media-post-ideas-for-business) guide.\\n\\n## Examples of Format Usage\\n\\nChoosing the right format depends entirely on what kind of content you are sharing. Here are practical examples of when to use which size.\\n\\n:::cards\\ntype: examples\\n\\n### The B2B Educational Carousel (Portrait 4:5)\\n**Format:** 1080 x 1350 px.\\n**Use Case:** A marketing agency sharing \\"5 Ways to Improve Local SEO\\". \\n**Why it works:** The extra vertical height provides room for a large headline, 3-4 bullet points per slide, and readable body text without making the design feel cramped.\\n\\n### The Product Launch Shot (Square 1:1)\\n**Format:** 1080 x 1080 px.\\n**Use Case:** An e-commerce brand announcing a new sneaker colorway.\\n**Why it works:** The square crop focuses the user's eye directly on the product in the center of the frame and looks neatly aligned on the brand's Instagram profile grid.\\n\\n### The Event Photography (Landscape 1.91:1)\\n**Format:** 1080 x 566 px.\\n**Use Case:** A tech company posting a wide shot of their team on stage at a conference.\\n**Why it works:** It preserves the context of the wide room and fits all team members into the frame without cropping anyone out, even if it sacrifices some vertical screen space.\\n:::\\n\\n## Practical Design Tips\\n\\n:::cards\\ntype: tips\\n\\n### Design for the swipe\\nWhen these dimensions are used for a multi-slide post, follow the [Instagram carousel workflow](/blog/how-to-make-an-instagram-carousel-with-ai) to organize the slide sequence as well as the canvas size.\\n\\nSince carousels are interactive, consider adding visual cues (like an arrow, a page number, or a continuous graphic that spans across the edge of the slide) to encourage the user to swipe to the next image.\\n\\n### Use portrait for text\\nIf your carousel contains educational text, lists, or step-by-step guides, always use the 1080x1350 portrait size. The extra vertical space allows you to use larger, more readable fonts for mobile users.\\n\\n### Check your text contrast\\nEnsure your text is easily readable on small screens. Use bold fonts and high contrast between the text and background colors. If people have to squint, they will keep scrolling.\\n:::\\n\\n## Common Formatting Mistakes\\n\\n:::cards\\ntype: mistakes\\n\\n### Mixing aspect ratios in a carousel\\nInstagram will force every slide in your carousel to match the aspect ratio of the first slide you select. If your cover slide is a square (1:1), all portrait (4:5) images behind it will be awkwardly cropped at the top and bottom.\\n\\n### Uploading low-resolution files\\nIf you upload an image smaller than 320 pixels wide, Instagram will stretch it, making it incredibly blurry. Always export your final designs at exactly 1080 pixels wide.\\n\\n### Putting text in the \\"Grid Danger Zones\\"\\nWhen a portrait (4:5) image is viewed on your profile grid, it is automatically cropped from the center to form a 1:1 square. If you put important text (like a headline) at the very top or bottom edge of your 4:5 design, it will be cut off when users view your overall profile grid.\\n:::\\n\\nFormatting and resizing images manually for every social network can be a tedious process. While a normal photo is easy to upload, a multi-slide educational post requires strict adherence to design rules, safe zones, and aspect ratios. If you regularly share expertise, lists, or step-by-step guides, an automated tool like GoToFlow can transform your raw text into a finished carousel with the perfect Instagram portrait dimensions applied automatically, saving you hours of design work.\\n"`);export{e as default};